_sha256" "b97218003d5040869cefa9df1b40539500482f73743419ed51cf01f56c45cb9e"} {"question" "q-17407" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039932/assets/b771ac7e3d7e724b1eae9ebfc592d36a903a1fc46cc2aac5a55ea9517dc2ce12.png)\n\nThe Argand diagram above shows the roots of a cubic polynomial $f(x).$\nFind $f(x)$ given that the coefficient of the cubic term is $1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039932/state.json" "file_sha256" "b97218003d5040869cefa9df1b40539500482f73743419ed51cf01f56c45cb9e"} {"question" "q-17407" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "From the diagram, we see that $f(x)$ has roots ${x}_{1}=4$ and ${x}_{2,3}=\\pm 3\\text{i}.$ Additionally, we're given that the coefficient of the cubic term is $a=1.$ Therefore, we can write the polynomial as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =a(x-{x}_{1})(x-{x}_{2})(x-{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =1(x-4)(x-3\\text{i})(x-(-3\\text{i})) \\\\  & =(x-4)(x-3\\text{i})(x+3\\text{i})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-4)(x-3\\text{i})(x+3\\text{i}) & = \\\\ (x-4)({x}^{2}-x(-3\\text{i})-x(3\\text{i})+(3\\text{i})(-3\\text{i})) & = \\\\ (x-4)({x}^{2}+9) & = \\\\ {x}^{3}+9x-4{x}^{2}-36 & = \\\\ {x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}+9x-36 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)={x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}+9x-36.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039932/state.json" "file_sha256" "b97218003d5040869cefa9df1b40539500482f73743419ed51cf01f56c45cb9e"} {"question" "q-17407" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039932/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256"